{"ts": "2026-10-26T20:19:19.319289Z", "run_id": "r-20261026T2019Z-2739", "unit": "openspec:add-architecture-fitness", "seq": 12, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-002", "target": "C-851f3c7c", "decision": "reject", "by": "delegate", "reason": "The scenario's THEN says each of the four fixture results also carries the egress-pin violations because the fixtures name no egress module, and design decision 6 pins the modules with an egress value to exactly observability, identity and gateway. The implementation does not deliver that: checkGraphIntegrity loops over Object.entries(modules) and consults PINNED_EGRESS only for rows that are present, so a graph with no observability, identity or gateway row (every one of the four fixtures, and a copy of MODULES with the gateway row deleted) yields no pin violation at all. On { a: { allowed: [\"zzz\"] } } it returns exactly one violation, the unknown-name one, and nothing about the pin. The passing test cannot see this because it asserts only with some on the needle and with every on code, file and line, never that a pin violation is present. The judge's major finding therefore stands. WHEN checkGraphIntegrity runs on a graph that lacks a row for observability, identity or gateway, such ", "text_sha256": "f88996b060b424a8dbc5ab062332b9d0eab072df70f441dd7a0540b4f0fb6c74", "answer_sha256": "674a9d44cac42d0cc8ebe1aa37fe428a3978b084e8a1ad96ba3d05c87261cbdb", "profile": "delegate-fable", "model": "claude-fable-5-1", "cli": "claude-code"}
{"ts": "2026-10-26T20:19:19.327370Z", "run_id": "r-20261026T2019Z-2739", "unit": "openspec:add-architecture-fitness", "seq": 13, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-003", "target": "C-c63269ba", "decision": "accept", "by": "agent", "reason": "scanText reports all eight kinds at their lines without echoing them, with the design.md minimum lengths. The test uses longer values than the minimum for three kinds, and the pem and aws patterns are a little broader than design.md (an extra space before the closing dashes, no guard after AKIA); both only add detections.", "text_sha256": "13fc2f4b6aa5fcb9a7b97cbc0ea6af4cf2667967d8a34ba2c8c6a3ae02422f21", "answer_sha256": "82ecb0b35aa8a69ae60964316d4fe84a19fd9bdd89fea168763fc16b4b63f098", "marker": "CLAUDECODE"}
{"ts": "2026-10-26T20:19:19.334609Z", "run_id": "r-20261026T2019Z-2739", "unit": "openspec:add-architecture-fitness", "seq": 14, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-004", "target": "C-eea53b80", "decision": "accept", "by": "agent", "reason": "a conduct criterion. On this unit checkAll(readSnapshot()) is empty (repo.test.ts passes), so no blocker was due, and the diff of the unit changes only test/arch and its openspec folders: nothing under src/ or test/support/ and no owner file.", "text_sha256": "34cf35f67573445aa8bf8eb947fb3d7c0abc53e3b321680b7551089774eaf97e", "answer_sha256": "83228367a7776c59b14a3782b86ff61a8d88074d2b83300b42ee6a147e5d3b28", "marker": "CLAUDECODE"}
{"ts": "2026-10-26T20:19:19.341748Z", "run_id": "r-20261026T2019Z-2739", "unit": "openspec:add-architecture-fitness", "seq": 15, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-005", "target": "C-8c4b4392", "decision": "accept", "by": "agent", "reason": "as Q-004. No directory of src/ is outside the graph at this head (repo.test.ts passes), so no ARCH_UNKNOWN_MODULE was raised and test/arch/modules.ts gained no row for one.", "text_sha256": "83109b59b7a16cbc4e16535b9b3931de468c3dc516112ba09ef5403cd22f82e4", "answer_sha256": "22d91f3b40b3299768214654791511e79f410242cf903440046e9202564306a3", "marker": "CLAUDECODE"}
{"ts": "2026-10-26T20:19:19.342477Z", "run_id": "r-20261026T2019Z-2739", "unit": "openspec:add-architecture-fitness", "seq": 16, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-006", "target": "C-ba84c521", "decision": "accept", "by": "delegate", "reason": "The criterion describes what the implementer does if a rule flags code that a passing scenario lists, and the repository shows that outcome for the scenario's own example. The network rule in test/arch/rules.ts skips every type-only import, and collectImports in test/arch/sources.ts marks import type declarations and all-type named bindings as type-only, so a type-only import of node:http in src/api is not reported. A test in network.test.ts asserts that checkNetwork returns an empty array for src/api/a.ts holding import type { Server } from \"node:http\" and import { type Server as Other } from \"node:http\", and a sources test asserts the typeOnly flags of every import form. The repository test asserts that checkAll over the real tree is an empty array, and no rule file contains a skip, todo or narrowed pattern, so no false positive was left standing and no blocker was raised. The criterion is satisfied as it stands.", "text_sha256": "665dcd99d7707e65054d42107ed534bba9ad079dfe7b58dba74e84d3639409b7", "answer_sha256": "a75af33c6da79d72182ce9f67cec4fda7e42686645c8d09de25949e75f73f6ab", "profile": "delegate-fable", "model": "claude-fable-5-1", "cli": "claude-code"}
{"ts": "2026-10-26T20:19:19.350094Z", "run_id": "r-20261026T2019Z-2739", "unit": "openspec:add-architecture-fitness", "seq": 17, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-007", "target": "F-a154400b", "decision": "accept", "by": "agent", "reason": "waived for this unit only. The second literal is how fix 1 answered F-35492b20: the base-test replay keeps test/arch/repo.test.ts at head (unfrozen by Q-001) and sets aside test/arch/modules.ts, a support file this unit adds, so a rules.ts that imports modules.ts cannot load in that run, and a fix as the reject asks would fail the same way. The import loads once modules.ts is on main; a follow-up right after this unit merges makes rules.ts read MODULES from modules.ts and drops the second literal.", "text_sha256": "a910a8067ba0e11cd70d47b47b425bcd3ef65873769d71ba8a1df5db72d92b62", "answer_sha256": "12b8248fa14a4dcd9caee040e1915d3f8df82b9bdad641dda149f565a180a727", "marker": "CLAUDECODE"}
{"ts": "2026-10-26T20:19:19.357483Z", "run_id": "r-20261026T2019Z-2739", "unit": "openspec:add-architecture-fitness", "seq": 18, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-008", "target": "F-a985c7e4", "decision": "accept", "by": "agent", "reason": "the same defect as Q-007, waived for the same reason; the follow-up after the merge makes test/arch/modules.ts the only literal of the graph and the default of every rule.", "text_sha256": "f1559c127072b60b50f39766b77c791fdc7ab0f8e9e39c33e53e9a429413d44c", "answer_sha256": "00fbdfc0b5bd54dedf5561c4c3c0e50b644240f4c7366bb3ca0793242be8b54a", "marker": "CLAUDECODE"}
{"ts": "2026-10-26T20:19:19.358162Z", "run_id": "r-20261026T2019Z-2739", "unit": "openspec:add-architecture-fitness", "seq": 19, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-009", "target": "F-ed69f6e7", "decision": "reject", "by": "delegate", "reason": "The finding is correct and the repository shows it. checkGraphIntegrity in test/arch/rules.ts iterates Object.entries(modules) and reads PINNED_EGRESS only for the row being visited; it never iterates PINNED_EGRESS itself. So a pinned module that is absent from the graph is never reported: on { a: { allowed: [\"zzz\"] } } the function returns exactly one violation, the unknown-name one, and on a copy of MODULES with the gateway row deleted it returns nothing about gateway. The missing-egress branch fires only when the row exists with egress undefined, which is why the shipped test that removes the egress property but keeps the gateway row still passes. The scenario \"Inconsistent graphs are reported\" says each of the four fixture results also carries the egress-pin violations because those fixtures name no egress module, and design decision 6 says the modules with an egress value are exactly observability, identity and gateway, a pin that a reviewed rule edit must guard. A graph that sile", "text_sha256": "9664cf20ae05cd8fd2736b55e5bc67814f40e5a05a7b28a9e7c5c6d72c75e08f", "answer_sha256": "66b3be88dcd5f517cb0b3812a36dce780a5c4e34e3dc3039864776a753781c8e", "profile": "delegate-fable", "model": "claude-fable-5-1", "cli": "claude-code"}
{"ts": "2026-10-26T20:19:19.359210Z", "run_id": "r-20261026T2019Z-2739", "unit": "openspec:add-architecture-fitness", "seq": 20, "type": "human.verdict", "question": "Q-010", "target": "F-85a00370", "decision": "reject", "by": "delegate", "reason": "The finding is correct and the repository shows it. namedBindingsAreTypeOnly in test/arch/sources.ts returns true when the clause is not itself type-only but its named bindings are a non-empty NamedImports list whose elements all carry the type modifier; it never looks at the ImportClause's default binding name. So for import https, { type Agent } from \"node:https\"; the clause has a default value binding, yet the ref is marked typeOnly, and checkNetwork in test/arch/rules.ts skips every ref whose typeOnly is true, so the import of node:https from a non-egress module is not reported, and the same holds for worker_threads. Design decision 3 makes a declaration type-only only for import type, export type from, an import type node, and a declaration with only named specifiers, at least one, all type-modified; a declaration with a default binding is not such a declaration and is a value import. WHEN collectImports reads an import declaration that has a default binding and named specifiers t", "text_sha256": "e89a189e12142ab33039f06a5d3176a55fb2fd375b974e4b506c7fd08b87dbd0", "answer_sha256": "7db5ef8935ed74c810532907b2369eb48fb2c526c4d2adfd7a42f45df98bc8ba", "profile": "delegate-fable", "model": "claude-fable-5-1", "cli": "claude-code"}
